# Every text file is LF in the index and LF in every working tree, whatever a clone's
# core.autocrlf says. Three things already depend on it and none of them would say so
# when it broke: the workflows run `git diff --exit-code` to prove the build idempotent,
# so a CRLF checkout reports every generator out of step; check:docs measures a document
# in characters, and a CR is one; and every reader of a generated file splits on '\n'
# and keeps the CR, so a comparison that should match differs by an invisible byte.
#
# This declares what the tree already is. `git ls-files --eol` reports LF for every text
# file here, so a renormalise is a no-op, and that is the point: the accident becomes a
# contract before a Windows clone gets the chance to disprove it. check:generated holds
# the other half, refusing a tracked text file that carries a CR at all.
* text=auto eol=lf

# A binary is diffed by nothing and converted by nothing. The .woff2 are the three git
# already detects; the rest are named ahead of the first one committed, because the cost
# of the omission is a corrupted file and the cost of the line is a line.
*.woff2 binary
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary

# An .svg is XML: a brand mark here is read and reviewed as text, and gets LF like the
# rest. Naming it is what keeps text=auto from having to guess.
*.svg   text eol=lf
